WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.560
It's 11 PM.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.830
You should be asleep, but you're watching

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.390
a video on your phone and
tomorrow you'll wake up,

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:09.930
and go to work, where you'll stare

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.090
at a computer for eight hours.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:16.540
When you get home, you might
even watch a movie on TV.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.410
So if you're anything like
the average American adult,

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.250
you spend more than seven hours a day

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.180
staring at digital screens.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.520
So what's all this screen
time actually doing

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.893
to your body and brain?

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.180
Surprise, surprise, humans
did not evolve to stare

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.360
at bright electronic screens all day

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.770
so our eyes are suffering
the consequences.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:46.440
An estimated 58% of people
who work on computers

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.420
experience what's called
computer vision syndrome.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.240
It's a series of symptoms
that include eye strain,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.960
blurred vision, headaches,
and neck and back pain.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.010
And in the longterm this
amount of screen time

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:02.480
could be damaging our vision permanently.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.470
Since 1971, cases of
nearsightedness in the US

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.740
have nearly doubled, which some scientists

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.440
partly link to increased screen time.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.240
And in Asia today, nearly 90%

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.930
of teens and adults are nearsighted.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.010
But it's not just the
brightness of our screens

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.320
that affect us, it's also the color.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.030
Screens emit a mix of red,
green, and blue light.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:26.840
Similar colors to sunlight.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:30.800
And over a millennia it was
blue wavelengths in sunlight

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.940
that helped us keep our circadian rhythms

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.710
in sync with our environment.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.150
But since our circadian
rhythms are more sensitive

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.130
to blue light than any
others, a problem occurs

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.050
when we use our screens at night.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.670
Typically when the sun sets

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.400
we produce the hormone melatonin.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.810
This hormone regulates
our circadian rhythms,

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.520
helping us feel tired and fall asleep.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.420
But many studies have
found that blue light

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.200
from screens can disrupt this process.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.440
For example, in one small study,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.250
participants who spent
four hours reading e-books

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.570
before bed for five nights,
produced 55% less melatonin

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.250
than participants that read print books.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.860
What's more, the e-book readers reported

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.380
that they were more alert before bed,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.360
took longer to fall asleep and
reach restorative REM state,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.100
and were more tired the next morning.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.430
But perhaps the most concerning changes

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.470
we're starting to see
from all this screen time

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.650
is in kid's brains.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.640
An ongoing study, supported by the NIH

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.000
has found that some preteens who clocked

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.930
over seven hours a day on screens

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.800
had differences in a part of
their brain called the cortex.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.370
That is the region responsible

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:42.960
for processing information
from our five senses.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.380
Usually the cortex gets
thinner as we mature,

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.180
but these kids had thinner
cortexes earlier than other kids

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.920
who spent less time on screens.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.930
Scientists aren't sure
what this could mean

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.100
for how the kids learn
and behave later in life,

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.860
but the same data also
showed that kids who spent

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.730
more than two hours a day on screens

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.880
scored lower in thinking
and in language skill tests.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.410
To be clear, the NIH data can't confirm

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:13.630
if more time spent staring at
screens causes these effects,

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.650
but they'll have a
better idea of any links

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.320
as they continue to follow and study

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.710
these kids over the next decade.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.670
It's no doubt that screens have changed

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.250
the way we communicate,
but only time will tell

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.763
what other changes are on
the horizon for human kind.

